Assistant: Perfect! All tests pass. Let me review the solution one more time to ensure it's correct and efficient:

The solution iterates over all possible pairs (x, y) where x != y (x for even indices, y for odd indices), and for each element computes the minimum operations needed to make its remainder equal to x or y.

For a number `num` with remainder `r = num % k`, and target remainder `t` (x or y), the minimal operations to make `num % k = t` is `min((r - t) % k, (t - r) % k)`. This works because:
- `(r - t) % k` is the distance going "down" (to the previous number ≡ t mod k)
- `(t - r) % k` is the distance going "up" (to the next number ≡ t mod k)
- The minimum of these two gives the closest ≡ t mod k value.

The algorithm tries all O(k²) pairs and for each pair computes O(n) operations, which is efficient since k ≤ 100 and n ≤ 100.

